WEBVTT
TITLE Arithmetic Algorithms Booth Division Cla — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A four-bit adder cannot multiply, cannot divide, and cannot add sixty-four bits in one step.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
A multiplier has only add and shift. Thirteen times eleven in four bits: three additions, four shifts.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
A divider has only subtract and shift: seven divided by three is two remainder one.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
And the adder itself is an algorithm: the ripple waits n levels, the lookahead waits two log two n plus two.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Two registers: A holds the running sum, Q holds the multiplier, and the pair shifts right together.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
Step one: Q zero is one, so add M into A, giving 01101, then shift.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Four steps later the product is 10001111, which is 143.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
The number of additions is the number of ones in the multiplier: three here, never more than n.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
A block of ones is a difference: 0111 is 1000 minus 0001, so it costs one subtraction and one addition.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
So look at two bits at a time: 01 adds M, 10 subtracts M, 00 and 11 do nothing.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
Recoding 1011 from the low end gives minus one, zero, plus one, minus one.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
Weighted by one, two, four and eight, those digits sum to minus five, which is the signed value of 1011.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
Three times minus four, in four bits: A is five bits, Q is 1100, and q minus one starts at zero.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
Steps one and two see the pair 00 twice: nothing is added, only the shift.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
Step three sees 10, the start of a run, so subtract M: A becomes 11101.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
Four steps and one subtraction give 11110100, which is minus twelve.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Here is the multiplier from GATE CSE 2025 Set 2, question 32: sixteen bits, written low bit first.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
Each change between neighbouring bits is one add or one subtract: thirteen changes.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
Thirteen is the answer, and the multiplicand never enters the count.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
Best case no operation at all, worst case four for a four-bit 0101: worse than plain shift-and-add.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Division, restoring version: M is 0011, the dividend seven sits in Q, and A starts at zero.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
Each step shifts left and tries A minus M. If the result is negative, add M back and set the quotient bit to zero.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
After four steps Q holds 0010 and A holds 0001: quotient two, remainder one.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
Three of the four steps had to restore, so half the work was undoing the algorithm's own subtractions.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
The non-restoring divider never undoes anything: it looks at the sign of A and adds M instead of subtracting.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
The remainder is allowed to go negative and stay negative for several steps.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Here A ends at minus two, so one correction adds M once, after the loop, and the remainder becomes one.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
Seven divided by four ends non-negative and needs no correction: the step is conditional, not automatic.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
A ripple adder waits: each carry is computed from the carry before it, so n bits take n levels.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Lookahead computes every carry from generate and propagate bits that are already available.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
On thirteen plus eleven the computed carries match ripple carry exactly, and the carry out is one.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
With two-input gates the depth is two log two n plus two levels, which is theta of log n: the 2016 question.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Trap one: forgetting the prepended q minus one bit, and answering twelve instead of thirteen.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Trap two: skipping the correction step in non-restoring division.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Trap three: calling a lookahead adder constant time when the gates have bounded fan-in.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
None of the three is arithmetic; each is a rule about the algorithm's own bookkeeping.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
If the question asks for a number of additions with a multiplier, count the transitions of Q.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
If it gives you a register trace, the pairs tell you the operation: add on 01, subtract on 10.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
For division, check both conditions at the end: the identity, and the range of the remainder.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
For a delay question, fix the gate fan-in first: two log base k of n plus two levels.

